// spec: VAR-006
//
// ⚠️ A defect is a statement about the INTERPRETER and is never compiled.
//
// ⚠️ THE 'Any' HALF IS FIXED.  A value declared Any no longer reaches a written
// type at an assignment any more than at a declaration -- both refuse it, and
// 'as' is how it earns the crossing.
//
// What remains is the UNTYPED half, and it is a genuine fork rather than an
// oversight.  VAR-006 refuses a value 'whose type could not be determined at
// all', and the checker cannot type ordinary locals in nested scopes:
//
//     var C := Copy (Text, I, 1);     -- untyped, though Copy returns a String
//     Result := Result + C;           -- refused if '' is refused here
//
// That line is in compiler/Scanner.a24 and it is correct code.  Refusing it
// punishes a program for the CHECKER's blind spot.  So an assignment permits
// '' and a declaration still refuses it -- which is the shape of the asymmetry
// this defect is named for, narrower.
//
// ⚠️ Resolving it wants better INFERENCE, not a looser rule: four tests of
// element-type scoping observe the checker only through the declaration's
// strictness, so removing that removes their ability to test at all.

function Untyped (); begin Exit 'x'; end

var S : String;
S := Untyped ();
WriteLn (S);
